{
  "image_name": "4dfa948ef99e86072d33aafcdce986ea.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how multiple problems in a transplanted or bioengineered tissue can drive failure outcomes such as calcification, organ dysfunction, and organ rejection. It links material-processing issues, poor healing, and immune triggers into one connected pathway.\n\nOn the left, excessive crosslinking reduces extracellular matrix hydrophilicity and cell function, disrupting matrix turnover and promoting calcification. Insufficient re-endothelialization appears to expose the surface to platelets, leading to platelet activation and clot formation. Other upstream insults, including contamination and detergents, cause cellular toxicity or damage and release danger signals.\n\nThe central part emphasizes innate immune activation. Contaminants and damaged cells release PAMPs and DAMPs, which are sensed by PRRs/TLRs on macrophages. Native cellular remnants, especially DNA, are shown being taken up by endocytosis and engaging TLR9, further stimulating inflammation.\n\nOn the right, residual immune targets provoke adaptive and humoral responses. Residual MHC-I is associated with incompatibility and activation of NK cells and CD8+ T cells, while α-Gal is recognized by anti-α-Gal antibodies, immune cells via Fc receptors, and the complement system. These pathways converge on toxic granule release, pro-inflammatory cytokines, complement mediators, severe inflammation, endothelial injury, and ultimately organ dysfunction or rejection.",
